 "engine": "Groq whisper-large-v3",
 "text": "If you ever need help in a crowd, don't yell, \"Somebody help!\" In 1968, Columbia psychologists filled a room with smoke. It poured in through a vent. When people were alone, 75% went to report it. But with two strangers who just kept working, only 10% did. They sat in the smoke, coughing, rubbing their eyes. Here's the trick. It's called the bystander effect. In a crowd, everyone waits for someone else. So point at one person. \"You, in the red shirt. Call 911.\" Now it's not somebody, it's them. Follow for more mind tricks.",
